REVIEWER REPORT(S):\
Referee: 1\
\
Comments to the Author\
The authors have performed an intensive study whereby several thousand \
MOF structures are screened for their suitability in performing the \
photocatalytic hydrogenation of CO2. The selection criteria for each \
stage of the screening, and their ordering, is rational and \
well-reasoned. The manuscript is well written, has a logical flow and \
features several well crafted and informative figures. The authors have \
made use of several well-established tools to establish a robust workflow.\
However, there are some serious matters for consideration, particularly \
with the DFT calculations, that prevent this paper from being suitable \
for publication.\
\'93Photo-hydrogenation combines the advantages of both methods to \
accelerate the overall catalytic process by using light to promote \
charge excitation and separation, lowering the required heat to carry \
out the reaction\'94.\
It is unclear what two methods are being referred to here. The process \
of excitation and separation is ubiquitous to all photocatalysis. \
Photo-hydrogenation appears to be a subset of photocatalytic reactions, \
just as electroreduction can be a subset of electrocatalysis. The \
authors frame photo-hydrogenation as a novel method that is entirely \
separate from photocatalysis, when that is not the case. Clarification \
is needed here. In Figure 1, the DFT analysis is shown to have taken 55 \
hrs and 30 mins. Is this the time taken to optimise a single \
intermediate, a full reaction profile on a single MOF candidate, or all \
reaction profiles on all selected MOF candidates?\
It should be specified whether DFT calculations are performed with spin \
polarisation.\
It is unclear to me what reaction conditions are being proposed for the \
photocatalytic CO2 conversion. Aqueous stability is screened for, \
implying a consideration for the presence of water. Are these materials \
intended to be immersed in an aqueous environment? Clarification on \
\'91humid conditions\'92 would be helpful.\
Regarding Equation 1, it appears that the energy of any intermediate is \
relative to the energy of a preceding intermediate, following a sort of \
cumulative scheme. However, it is unclear to me why such a method is \
chosen for calculating the relative energies. Standard practice is to \
calculate all energies relative to a common reference, i.e., the clean \
and relaxed MOF and gas phase species. \uc0\u8710 E=E_(MOF+ads)-E_MOF-\u12310 xE\u12311 _(CO_2 \
(g))-yE_(H_2 (g))\
Looking at the authors proposed scheme, it may be that the adsorbate is \
removed from the MOF at n-1 and a single point calculation is performed \
on the resulting MOF configuration, but the point remains the same, that \
the energies appear to not have a common reference.\
Ground state energies were calculated at the DFT-D3 level. However, in \
the introduction the authors claim that the photo-hydrogenation \
mechanism relies on charge excitation and separation. The photocatalytic \
mechanism for CO2 hydrogenation involves the interaction of CO2 with an \
excited electron to give the CO2\'96 intermediate.(1) The authors have \
modelled the photocatalytic mechanism as a thermocatalytic mechanism. \
The authors have not accounted for or discussed any excitation in the \
calculation of the reaction profile. How is their ground state reaction \
profile linked to a process involving an excited state?\
The authors also state, \'93One exception is qmof-52ef7e9, which exhibits a \
slight endothermic step at initial CO2 adsorption before proceeding \
exothermically through the remaining steps, though this barrier should \
be overcome by the hydrogenation reaction conditions.\'94 What conditions \
are being referred to here?\
It should be made clear that all reaction steps involve the concerted \
hydrogenation of CO2 intermediates. Is H2 intended to be a feedstock? Or \
are holes used to oxidise water and generate protons? Justification is \
required for not modelling individual PCET steps.\
It is unclear if the authors have sampled different configurations for \
each step. i.e., Have they tried to coordinate CO2 to the metal centre \
via the C atom, or have they attempted to hydrogenate the oxygen atom \
that is coordinated to the metal centre?\
The authors have screened 20,000 MOF structures using a correction to \
account for the well-known failing of GGA methods to reproduce the \
bandgap. While appropriate for such a large number of structures, the \
authors should validate the bandgaps of their successful candidate \
materials using a more accurate hybrid functional. This is a necessary \
sanity check for their workflow.\
All DFT energies in the SI should be in eV, as that is the convention \
used in the main text.\
Figures in the SI should also have captions. It is unclear what is being \
measured in Table/Figure S7.\
References\
(1) Ingham, M., Aziz, A., Di Tommaso, D., Crespo-Otero, R. Mater. Adv. \
2023, 4 (22), 5388\'965419.\
\
\
Referee: 2\
\
Comments to the Author\
The article \'93In Silico Screening of Metal-Organic Frameworks for CO2 \
Photo-Hydrogenation\'94 by Moin Khwaja, Adrian Loy Chun Minh, and Takuya \
Harada reports a study that is highly relevant for the circular carbon \
economy. This study illustrates the application of high-throughput \
theoretical screening in applied research. The implemented protocol can \
also be applied to other similar applications. Additionally, the \
material cost analysis is a good inclusion, as the authors are also \
considering economic feasibility.\
However, the study lacks scientific discussion, and the results are \
reported as they are obtained. The authors didn\'92t sufficiently discuss \
the reasons for the differences in observations among the various \
candidates they are considering. Such a discussion is needed, at least \
for the 8 MOFs that were finally screened.\
For the DFT analysis, the authors performed thermodynamic screening and \
duly noted that the kinetic barriers were not calculated. Given the \
computations performed in this manuscript, it is necessary to include a \
kinetic analysis to strengthen the conclusions. If the aim is to reduce \
computational cost, the kinetic barriers (via Cl-NEB) can be performed \
on the last 5 novel materials they have identified for experimental \
research.\
There are major concerns about the calculation of the total energy (in \
eq 1). Which energy is used from the DFT results? Do they consist of \
only DFT energy, or is there an inclusion of ZPE and entropy \
contribution? If they are included, it should also be mentioned in the \
computation section. If they are not included, they should also be added \
to the energy values for more accurate results.\
Additionally, how are the ground state geometries confirmed after SCF \
analysis? How are the active sites and stable geometries of adsorbents \
identified? Data on the screening of these should also be provided (at \
least in the SI file) on adsorption energies for different active sites \
and orientations. Also, Ref 59 is not found; check it and correct it. \
So, the manuscript in its current form is not suitable for publication \
in Nanoscale, but with these additional results and detailed \
discussions, it will be suitable for publication.\
The following are suggestions for improving clarity and ease of reading.\
1. In Figure 4, instead of Frame 2a\'85.. in the X-axis, give the reaction \
which is referred (Eg, CO2(ads)/*CO2)\
2. In Table S5, instead of total energy, change it to relative energy. \
Also, the column title can also be modified similar to Figure 4, instead \
of add H2, add co2 \'85.\
3. In Figure 6, instead of using different gradients of green, use three \
distinct colors in the graph.\
4. Table 1 should be given before section 4, as the table is first \
mentioned in section 3.2.\
5. While citing Websites, the date accessed should also be included.\
\
